 author_note='Working draft, 27 October 2026. Author transliterations, remaining authors and corresponding author require confirmation.'
 abstract=('Predicting perturbation responses in primary immune cells requires distinguishing access to supporting experiments from improvements in model architecture. We evaluate FVcell, a low-rank shared-response and context-residual predictor, using frozen response permissions, strong simple comparators, four donor holdouts, and a separate cell-line study. The primary analysis contains 33,258 target-condition responses across 10,282 published genes; donor-resolved analyses use 129,671 aggregates across 18,129 reference features. On pooled held-out combinations, FVcell reaches a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.173411, compared with 0.173536 for concatenation and 0.172030 for a shrunk additive rule. Learned hierarchical shrinkage does not materially improve four-donor error over fixed Gaussian shrinkage or an equally continued shared-residual model. In the independent study, covering 519 shared targets and 6,795 shared genes in K562 and RPE1, FVcell has MAE 0.232618 versus 0.227809 for zero response. Its within-cell-line target retrieval is 0.096%, whereas a source target-mean lookup retrieves 9.056% despite a worse MAE. Guide-disjoint measurement checks have low agreement on the small eligible subset, limiting attribution of this failure. These results support an evidence-qualified evaluation of terminal response prediction, rather than a claim of broadly validated virtual-cell simulation. We provide explicit information boundaries, matched compute controls, and auditable failure cases; we do not establish a new state-of-the-art algorithm or clinical validity.')
 paper=[]
 paper.append(page({'kind':'title','text':'FVcell: Evidence Access and Transfer Limits in T-Cell Perturbation Prediction'}, {'kind':'authors','text':'Di Zhu; Yao Zhao\nFudan University'},text(author_note),heading('Abstract'),text(abstract),heading('1  Introduction'),text('An accurate virtual-cell predictor should estimate how an intervention changes a biological system in a specified context. A model that recognizes an average expression profile is solving a different problem from one that recovers the signed, intervention-specific change. Primary T cells are a demanding setting because stimulation, donor background, guide efficacy and assay design affect the measured response. Large perturbation resources now make these distinctions testable [1,2].'),text('Our starting hypothesis was that a shared perturbation program, supplemented by a context-dependent low-rank residual and hierarchical shrinkage, would improve transfer under sparse supervision. We test that hypothesis on the existing FVcell implementation rather than replacing unfavorable outcomes with a different claim. The contribution is an explicit evaluation of evidence permissions and transfer limits for this implementation. The individual architectural and statistical components have established precedents; their presence alone is not a methodological advance.'),text('We ask three questions: whether response evidence can be separated from architectural gains; whether learned hierarchical pooling improves on fixed shrinkage and matched optimization; and whether favorable within-study error transfers to an independently sourced experiment. The resulting answer is qualified: same-target evidence changes the task, hierarchical improvements are negligible, and the available external cell-line test does not support broad transfer.')))
 paper.append(page(heading('2  Related work and data'),text('Recent evidence cautions against assuming that complex predictors improve perturbation response estimation. Ahlmann-Eltze and colleagues report strong simple linear baselines [3], while Systema separates perturbation-specific prediction from systematic variation [4]. Calibrated metrics can reveal benefits that a single expression score misses [5]. We therefore report error, signed association, amplitude and perturbation identity together; we do not claim to reproduce a published noise-calibration estimator.'),text('The nearest conceptual precedents are especially important. The recoverable-resolution preprint already studies shared and context-specific effects and donor-aware evaluation [6]. Mechanisms Matter studies transportability, including primary T-cell settings [7]. PIE uses perturbation and context evidence for generalization [8]. Our implementation is not the first shared-residual decomposition, evidence-conditioning strategy or four-donor evaluation. State provides a distinct response-prediction system [9]; the comparison here uses an endpoint adapter of its official pseudobulk backbone, not the full pretrained State workflow.'),heading('2.1  Source response data'),text('We use the public GWCD4i processed files associated with Zhu et al. [1]. The pooled DESeq2 matrix contains 33,983 rows and 10,282 output genes; 33,258 eligible rows represent 11,086 targets across Rest, Stim8hr and Stim48hr. Its upstream gene and target curation is inherited from the data release. Separately, the guide/donor pseudobulk file contains 278,684 rows and 18,129 reference features. Raw-count QC and aggregation produce 129,671 eligible target-donor-context responses. These units are analyzed separately: a pooled DESeq2 log2 fold change is not interchangeable with a donor-resolved log2-CPM contrast.'),table(['Analysis','Biological unit','Outputs','Main shift'],[['Pooled combinations','Published pooled contrast','10,282','Target x culture'],['Donor holdouts','Donor x target x culture','18,129','One of four donors'],['External study','Cell-line x target aggregate','6,795 shared','Study and cell background']],[104,156,90,118], 'Table 1. Distinct estimands are reported in separate score blocks.'),heading('2.2  Reference controls and provenance'),text('Donor-level labels are log2(1 + CPM_target) minus log2(1 + CPM_NTC), after summing counts over eligible guides and sequencing runs within each donor and culture. The library denominator includes all 18,129 published reference features. Eligible source pseudobulks have at least five cells, positive library totals and finite nonnegative counts. We do not select guides by observed effectiveness or genes by differential-expression significance. Input versions, frozen assignments, output masks and checkpoint hashes are retained in the accompanying audit records.'),text('The four source donors are CE0006864, CE0008162, CE0008678 and CE0010866. Millions of cells do not create millions of independent donors. All donor comparisons retain the person as the biological unit, and seed variability is reported as optimization variability.')))
 paper.append(page(heading('3  FVcell and evidence permissions'),heading('3.1  Terminal response operator'),text('Let y_(d,c,a) denote a measured response vector for donor d, culture c and target a. The model receives an action representation u_a and an allowed reference-state representation z_(d,c). It predicts a signed terminal response using a shared program and a low-rank interaction:'),equation('delta_hat = mu_train + W_s h(u) + W_r [A(u) * tanh(S(z) + b_c + v_d)]'),text('Here * is elementwise multiplication; h is a two-layer 65-to-64-to-32 action network, A maps 65 inputs to 32 latent dimensions, and S maps 35 state inputs to 32 dimensions. Two output heads map the latent programs to genes. The source training response mean is added outside the network. The unaugmented model omits b_c and v_d. There is no nonnegative output activation, because responses may increase or decrease.'),text('The first 32 action coordinates encode unsigned Reactome pathway membership using fixed, reproducible random pathway vectors, normalized by target degree. Isolated targets receive deterministic ID vectors. These annotations describe membership, not a regulatory direction. The next 32 coordinates optionally project permitted same-target response evidence; the final coordinate flags whether that evidence exists. The state representation projects a matched NTC expression vector to 32 dimensions and appends three culture coordinates. Feature centering and scaling use training rows only.'),heading('3.2  Information regimes'),table(['Regime','Reference state','Response-derived numeric input'],[['P0','Culture metadata only','Disabled'],['P1','Matched untreated/NTC reference','Disabled'],['P2','As P1','Permitted source same-target responses']],[57,184,227], 'Table 2. Response evidence is an input permission, separate from the architecture.'),text('Initial input-permission comparisons keep their original supervision and are labeled accordingly. A stricter matched-supervision variant removes held-out target-response rows from all supervised contexts, then permits other-context rows only as explicitly logged evidence under P2. Validation and test responses are excluded from evidence construction. Training examples omit their own response when computing the numeric same-target feature. An observed target in a new context is not a globally unseen target.'),heading('3.3  Regularization and categorical paths'),text('Training minimizes a SmoothL1 response loss with residual shrinkage and a pathway-geometry penalty comparing latent and annotation similarities. Artificial missing-label experiments use explicit observation masks; hidden entries contribute no loss or gradient. The hierarchy adds zero-sum culture and training-donor latent offsets, with Gaussian penalties and learned log scales partially pooled toward a global scale. This is a regularized MAP-style fit, not posterior sampling. An unobserved donor has offset exactly zero.'),text('Culture one-hot weights and culture offsets can represent the same shift. We remove that duplicate categorical path, absorb its fitted contribution into centered offsets and the global bias, and continue training. The transformation is checked on training predictions. This removes one implementation redundancy; it does not prove global neural-network identifiability. Fixed-Gaussian and unshrunk-offset controls receive the same correction.')))
 paper.append(page(heading('4  Experimental protocol'),heading('4.1  Splits, tuning and compute controls'),text('Target-condition combination splits, globally held-out target splits and donor assignments are frozen before each corresponding experiment registry. In the four donor folds, one donor is held out for testing and another supplies validation, leaving two training donors. Validation targets are divided by a fixed hash into model-selection and interval-calibration halves. Neither the calibration half nor test responses select learning rates, shrinkage or checkpoints. Some initial test summaries were already known before later extensions; those extensions are exploratory rather than a new preregistered confirmation.'),text('Neural models use seeds 11, 29 and 47; AdamW; batch size 128; learning rates 0.001 and 0.0003; at most 3,000 steps; and validation every 250 steps with patience four. The first seed selects a learning rate on the selection half and the remaining seeds inherit it. Hierarchical shrinkage strengths are 0.0003 and 0.003. The categorical-path correction adds at most 1,000 steps, and the unaugmented FV model receives a matched continuation. Seed means are descriptive optimization summaries, not biological confidence bounds.'),text('Simple controls are zero response, the source training mean, a source same-target mean, ridge regression and a shrunk additive rule. The additive rule shrinks each target mean toward the global training mean and adds a culture program. Its two weights are selected from {0, 0.25, 0.5, 1} on the source selection half only. Concatenation uses the same allowed action and context features; its hidden width is chosen to give a similar parameter budget. The State comparison is explicitly the adapted official backbone. Mechanism, residual, state and prior-strength ablations retain their stated data and permission setting.'),heading('4.2  Metrics and uncertainty'),equation('MAE = mean_d mean_c mean_a mean_g |delta_(d,c,a,g) - delta_hat_(d,c,a,g)|'),text('MAE is aggregated equally over targets within culture and then equally over cultures and donors. Each label unit has its own score block. Secondary measures include centered Pearson correlation of the signed response, direction accuracy among measured genes with absolute response greater than 0.1, and truth-aligned amplitude, dot(prediction, truth) / dot(truth, truth). Pearson correlation is undefined for a zero vector, rather than assigned zero. Zero response receives zero direction accuracy on nonzero truth.'),text('Within-condition perturbation retrieval ranks all eligible measured target responses by cosine similarity to a prediction. Equal similarities receive average ranks and expected top-1 credit under random tie resolution. This prevents constant predictions from receiving arbitrary wins. Pooled differential-expression average precision uses finite published adjusted P values <= 0.1 as positives and absolute predicted fold change as the score; there is no extra fold-change cutoff. We do not derive donor-level DE labels from pooled significance.'),text('Interval calibration reports both whole-response coverage and width. Under donor or study shift, exchangeability is not established, so empirical coverage is not a distribution-free guarantee. Four-donor contrasts and target-cluster bootstraps are descriptive. Repeated cells, guides, contexts and optimization seeds are not treated as independent people.')))
 pooledrows=[[label,f'{score("E1_P2_combo",fam):.6f}'] for label,fam in [('Zero response','zero'),('Training mean','training_mean'),('Same-target mean','same_target_mean'),('Shrunk additive','shrunk_additive'),('Ridge','ridge'),('Concatenation','concat'),('FVcell, original','fv')]]
 paper.append(page(heading('5  Within-study results'),heading('5.1  Simple shrinkage remains the strongest pooled comparator'),table(['Model','Pooled P2 MAE'],pooledrows,[320,148], 'Table 3. Same held-out combination task and DESeq2 label unit. Neural entries average three seeds.'),figure(F/'pooled_mae.png','Figure 1. The shared-residual model improves slightly over concatenation but remains behind the shrunk additive rule. Axis limits are restricted to show these small differences.'),text('The original FV model improves over concatenation by 0.000125 MAE, approximately 0.072% of concatenation error. This is a small implementation-level comparison. The simple shrunk additive rule improves on FV by 0.001381 MAE and therefore defeats a broad claim that the proposed neural factorization is the best predictor on this task. The corrected hierarchical pooled model reaches 0.173392, while equally continued FV reaches 0.173386; neither reverses this ordering against the additive rule.'),heading('5.2  Evidence access changes the prediction problem'),text('Under strict matched supervision, FV has MAE 0.176230 at P0, 0.176216 at P1, and 0.173655 at P2. Thus, the observed benefit of numeric same-target evidence is much larger than the original FV-versus-concatenation difference. This comparison measures information access as well as learning: P2 supplies response measurements in other admissible contexts. It must not be described as response-free prediction of an entirely unseen target.'),text('Secondary pooled metrics also constrain interpretation. The corrected hierarchical model has signed-response Pearson correlation approximately 0.238, direction accuracy approximately 0.578, and DE average precision approximately 0.0127 against a positive prevalence of 0.00647. These enrichments do not establish accurate effect amplitudes or functional phenotypes. Reporting only the small main-error improvement over one neural comparator would obscure both the stronger additive baseline and the information-permission effect.')))
 dr=[[{'fixed_gaussian':'Fixed Gaussian','fv_continued':'FV, continued','hierarchical':'Hierarchical','unshrunk_offsets':'Unshrunk offsets'}[f],f'{donor.loc[f,"mae"]:.9f}',f'{donor.loc[f,"signed_Pearson"]:.4f}'] for f in ['fixed_gaussian','fv_continued','hierarchical','unshrunk_offsets']]
 paper.append(page(heading('6  Donor generalization and supported resolution'),table(['Model','Four-donor MAE','Signed Pearson'],dr,[218,143,107],'Table 4. Equal-weight donor means, using donor-resolved log2-CPM contrasts. Overlapping folds are not independent studies.'),figure(F/'donor_mae.png','Figure 2. Held-donor variation is much larger than the differences between hierarchy and compute-matched controls; curves nearly overlap.'),text('Hierarchical pooling yields mean MAE 0.230135943, compared with 0.230136358 for fixed Gaussian shrinkage and 0.230127791 for continued FV. The fixed-minus-hierarchical reduction is 0.000000415; its descriptive four-donor bootstrap interval is [-0.000000133, 0.000001054]. Exact enumeration over the 16 donor sign patterns gives P = 0.375, or 0.75 after the exploratory Holm adjustment used in the audit. With four donors and overlapping training folds, these statistics do not provide reliable population-level precision. They do not support a material hierarchical gain.'),heading('6.1  New targets, missing labels and interval calibration'),text('The corrected global-target models have MAE 0.174383133 for hierarchy and 0.174383771 for continued FV, again an effectively negligible difference. Artificial 70%- and 30%-observed training-label experiments, full held-culture removals and mechanism/state ablations are retained in Supplementary All Model Results. Missing-label tests evaluate robustness to a fixed deletion mechanism; they are not experimental validation of missing biological responses. None of these branches establishes the originally proposed multi-organ response task.'),text('Empirical 90% whole-response interval coverage ranges approximately from 0.825 to 0.956, with average widths approximately 25.7 to 35.2 in the response unit. Coordinate-level coverage exceeds 0.99999. Wide intervals can cover most coordinates while offering little practical resolution. These are empirical cross-donor checks, not Bayesian credible intervals or guaranteed out-of-distribution coverage.'),heading('6.2  Functional measurements are an additional assay, not a new study'),text('The IL-10/IL-21 panel supplies 75 matched measurements on nine preselected targets and 13 additional donors whose identifiers are disjoint from the four transcriptomic donors. RNA responses for the nine targets are removed from training and selection. All six target-level correlation intervals include zero, so the panel does not provide reliable functional confirmation. Its donors are additional, but the assay belongs to the source study and was selected by that study. We therefore distinguish an independent readout from an independently sourced experiment.')))
 erows=[[{'zero':'Zero','training_mean':'Training mean','fv':'FVcell','state_official':'State backbone adapter','ridge':'Ridge','concat':'Concatenation','same_target_mean':'Source target lookup'}[n],f'{external[n]["mae"]:.6f}',('Undefined' if external[n]['pearson'] is None else f'{external[n]["pearson"]:.4f}'),f'{external[n]["retrieval_top1"]*100:.3f}'] for n in ['zero','training_mean','fv','state_official','ridge','concat','same_target_mean']]
 paper.append(page(heading('7  Independent-study transfer audit'),heading('7.1  A held-out study with a strong biological shift'),text('The independent experiment is Replogle et al. [2], using harmonized K562 and RPE1 count matrices. All response observations from both cell backgrounds are held out from fitting and model selection. The source refit uses only primary-T supervision and the existing frozen protocol. The overlap contains 519 targets, 6,795 shared published retained genes, and 1,038 test responses. NTC references are permitted; external perturbation responses are not features. Targets may be present in source training. This is a study-and-cell-background shift, not a new-target test, a matched primary-T replication or a test of unseen people.'),table(['Model','MAE','Pearson','Top-1 (%)'],erows,[202,89,87,90],'Table 5. External target retrieval has 519 candidates per cell line; random tied retrieval is 0.193%. Neural values average seed-specific scores.'),figure(F/'external_tradeoff.png','Figure 3. Error and perturbation identity give different orderings. The target lookup retains more identity despite a worse average expression error.'),text(f'FV error exceeds zero-response error by {pair["zero"]["FV_minus_comparator_MAE"]:.6f}. Its descriptive paired target-bootstrap interval is [0.004556, 0.005056]. FV has low truth-aligned amplitude (0.00956) and retrieval below the tied-random reference, consistent with severe shrinkage and weak target discrimination in this transfer setup. Source target lookup retrieves 9.056% while its MAE is 0.266788. These observations show why favorable error relative to another neural model cannot by itself establish useful intervention-specific transfer.'),text('The fixed external context encoder hashes literal context names into three coordinates. It is reproducible and shared by all evaluated source refits, but it provides no semantic model of a new cell type. Assay retention, culture, guide design and biological background also differ. Failure in this setup cannot be assigned uniquely to the shared-residual architecture.')))
 paper.append(page(heading('7.2  Measurement and negative controls'),text('Guide-disjoint target/control aggregates are feasible for only 31 K562 and 32 RPE1 targets at the predefined minimum of five cells per half. Mean signed-response agreement is 0.0081 and 0.0440, respectively. This sparse subset is not a study-wide noise ceiling, and guide splits are not donor replicates. Its low agreement limits interpretation of a model-versus-gold failure as a clean estimate of biological transportability.'),text('Twenty wrong-target derangements are retained with fixed random seeds. Matched and deranged predictions are compared using the same seed-mean ensemble, avoiding a seed-mean-versus-ensemble mismatch. The resulting control files distinguish generic response structure from target identity. These controls diagnose this implementation and data configuration; they do not prove that primary-T responses are intrinsically unlearnable.'),heading('8  Discussion and limitations'),text('The tested framework exposes three different sources of apparent progress: richer input evidence, additional optimization, and a change in the evaluated biological task. P2 evidence and P1 annotation-only input are different permissions even when their outputs share the name “unseen combination.” Hierarchical shrinkage and extra training must be compared with equally continued simple predictors. Low average response error must be checked against zero response, amplitude and target identification.'),text('Our evidence does not support superiority over the strongest simple pooled baseline, a meaningful learned-hierarchy advantage, robust functional validation, or broad cell-type transfer. This negative conclusion is specific to the implementation, processed resources and metrics tested. The donor cohort has only four individuals; the external experiment changes cell background and assay as well as study; source retained-gene sets differ; and some test summaries were known before subsequent exploratory analyses. A favorable or unfavorable score on these data is not a statement about every virtual-cell model.'),text('The methodological novelty is limited by direct prior work on response decomposition, transportability and evidence conditioning [6-8]. We present the audit as a reproducible case study, not as proof that these components are new. A stronger computational-methods submission would require an additional, clearly distinct technical contribution and evidence of its value. Additional training alone cannot create that contribution or substitute for matched independent primary-T measurements.'),text('The current model estimates terminal aggregate responses. It does not recover single-cell response distributions, temporal trajectories, tissue trafficking, composition changes, causal mediation or clinical outcomes. A tissue-average expression change can reflect both within-type responses and changing cell proportions; the present experiments do not identify those components. Multi-organ simulation remains outside the demonstrated scope.'),heading('9  Reproducibility and availability'),text('The local research package contains frozen response permissions, data receipts, row assignments, feature and checkpoint hashes, explicit observation masks, all method-task summaries, seed-level external scores and matched controls. The external audit reconstructs existing predictions and verifies agreement without retraining. Complete scalar result tables accompany this draft. Public source datasets remain subject to their original terms. Code and README are prepared locally; a public archival release and its permanent identifier are not yet available. This is a working manuscript, not a claim that submission or public release has occurred.'),text('A further same-modality primary-T study (GSE278572) has been identified and its full count matrix is being acquired under a separate frozen protocol. Its scores are not reported here. The closer genome-scale independent resource at Zenodo 18876252 is embargoed and contributes no test labels. Neither planned analysis is counted as completed validation.')))
